<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Dewas Senior</h1><p class="st-meta">1728 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Dewas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The senior of the two Maratha Puar states that shared the town of Dewas in Malwa, remembered through E. M. Forster's The Hill of Devi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukoji-rao-i-puar">Tukoji Rao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jivaji-rao-puar">Jivaji Rao Puar</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a> around 1728. Their shared holding developed into two states, the Senior Branch descending from Tukoji Rao and the Junior from Jivaji Rao, which divided land and revenues but kept the same capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a> Senior was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-india-agency">Central India Agency</a>, centred on the hill shrine of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamunda">Chamunda</a> above the town. Each branch had its own palace, administration and quarter of Dewas. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-m-forster">E. M. Forster</a> served as secretary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukoji-rao-iii-of-dewas-senior">Tukoji Rao III</a> in 1921, an experience he described in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hill-of-devi">The Hill of Devi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a> Senior merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madhya-bharat">Madhya Bharat</a> in 1948, which became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dewas-senior.jpg" alt="Dewas Senior" width="320" height="202"><figcaption>Image: QuadrangleDinglePingle · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Dewas_Senior_(Princely_State).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1728 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dewas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tukoji Rao I, Tukoji Rao III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marathi, Malvi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dewas State (Senior Branch)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=dewas-senior" aria-label="Dewas Senior on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6712 1330 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dewas Senior lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7032 1586L6991 1757M7032 1586L7364 1601M7032 1586L7364 1602M7032 1586L7064 1471"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6991 1757h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7032 1586h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7032 1586h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=dewas-senior">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
